===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|originally|US|informal|derogatory}} A [[person]] of [[meek]] or [[timid]] [[disposition]]; a person who [[lack#Verb|lacks]] [[character]] or [[effectiveness]].
#: {{synonyms|en|softy|wimp|wuss|Thesaurus:milksop}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Alexandra Kropotkin|title=To the Ladies|editor={{w|Fulton Oursler}}|magazine=[[w:Liberty (general interest magazine)|Liberty]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Liberty Publishing|date=21 October 1939|volume=16|issue=42|page=51|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/LibertyV16N4219391021/page/n50/mode/1up|column=1|oclc=72942004|passage=The '''Milquetoasts''' of this world seldom wake up in time to get tough effectively, Mr. [[w:H. T. Webster|[Harold Tucker] Webster]] fears. [...] Webster considers him a worthy citizen—of the kind that had better begin to show a little more spunk before it is too late.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Muriel Larson|title=Me and My Pet Peeves|location=Nashville, Tenn.|publisher=[[w:LifeWay Christian Resources|Broadman Press]]|year=1988|page=79|isbn=978-0-8054-5060-6|passage=The '''Milquetoasts''' of this world are the victims of the steamrollers. I used to be numbered among the '''Milquetoasts''' and was periodically used or flattened! I came to realize, however, that even though Christians are to be humble and cooperative, they also are accountable to God to use their time wisely, to do what He wants them to do, and to stand up for what is right and biblical.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Richard M. Fried|chapter=Two Eras, and Some Victims|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=rwaYC7txB2AC&pg=PT80|title=Nightmare in Red: The McCarthy Era in Perspective|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Oxford University Press}}|year=1990|isbn=978-0-19-504360-0|edition2=paperback|location2=New York, N.Y.|publisher2=Oxford University Press|year2=1991|isbn2=978-0-19-504361-7|passage=That scientists favored international control of the atom made conservatives even more mistrustful. [[w:J. Parnell Thomas|J[ohn] Parnell Thomas]] rued the surrender of control to such "a group of '''milktoasts'''."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Frank Manchel|chapter=A Representative Period of American Film (1913–1919)|title=Film Study: An Analytical Bibliography|volume=2|location=Rutherford; Madison, N.J.|publisher={{w|Fairleigh Dickinson University Press}}; London; Toronto, Ont.: {{w|Associated University Presses}}|year=1990|page=1400|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=ID4E3Lm8TsgC&pg=PA1400|isbn=978-0-8386-3412-7|passage=Of the thirteen films he &#91;{{w|Douglas Fairbanks}}&#93; made independently, the most noteworthy was [[w:Wild and Woolly|WILD AND WOOLLY]] (1917), a melodramatic story about an Eastern '''milquetoast''' who becomes a frontier hero.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Louis Baldwin|chapter=Clare Boothe Luce: Powerful Politician (1903–1987)|title=Women of Strength: Biographies of 106 who have Excelled in Traditional Male Fields, A.D. 61 to the Present|location=Jefferson, N.C.; London|publisher={{w|McFarland & Company}}|year=1996|page=42|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=J5JIY4RTq3YC&pg=PA42|isbn=978-0-7864-0250-2|passage=&#91;{{w|Clare Boothe Luce}}&#93; was no woman to be married to a '''milquetoast''', and [[w:Henry Luce|Henry R[obinson] Luce]], cofounder and colorful, forceful honcho of the nascent ''Time'' empire, was anything but that.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Elizabeth A. Ford|author2=Deborah C. Mitchell|chapter=The Ladder of Class|title=The Makeover in Movies: Before and After in Hollywood Films, 1941–2002|location=Jefferson, N.C.|publisher={{w|McFarland & Company}}|year=2004|page=91|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Jmz__lna90kC&pg=PA91|isbn=978-0-7864-1721-6|passage=Initially, our heroine hooks up with some '''milktoast''' of a guy with lots of money and little personality. Then ''he'' comes along—a man she can't push over, a man her wealth and social position can't influence.}}

